const sections=[{title:'Assessment before treatment',paragraphs:['Low mood, loss of interest, sleep or appetite changes, hopelessness and reduced functioning deserve careful assessment. A webpage cannot diagnose depression. These experiences can arise in different contexts, and each person’s history, health and circumstances matter. A clinician may ask when the change began, how it affects everyday life and whether there are periods when mood or energy feel different.','Before a first conversation, consider noting the main changes you have noticed, relevant medicines or health conditions, previous support and the question you most want answered. You do not need to arrive with a complete timeline or decide which treatment you want. The assessment is a chance to discuss what is happening and what options may be appropriate.']},{title:'Possible next steps',paragraphs:['Depending on the assessment, support may include follow-up, therapy, medication discussion, additional evaluation, practical changes or referral. No single plan fits everyone. Ask what a suggested option is intended to help with, what benefits and risks are relevant, what alternatives exist and when the plan will be reviewed. A prescription or particular outcome is not automatic.','For medical assessment or medication questions, see <a href="/psychiatry">psychiatry consultations</a>. For psychological support, read about <a href="/therapy">clinical psychology and therapy</a>. Some people use both services. The <a href="/psychiatrist-vs-psychologist">care-role guide</a> explains the difference, and the guide to <a href="/blog/pages/adult/psychiatrist-vs-psychologist-depression">psychiatry or psychology for depression</a> explores where to begin.']},{title:'Safety',paragraphs:['If there is immediate danger, call 112 or attend the nearest emergency department. If someone feels unable to stay safe, do not wait for a routine appointment or a reply to an online enquiry. Mind Grace is not an emergency service; 